Find the second-highest distinct number
Problem statement

Find the second-largest distinct number in an array.

Input:
int[] arr = {10, 20, 5, 40, 30};


Expected output:
30


A. Approach and logic
We maintain two variables:
- highest: Stores the largest number.
- secondHighest: Stores the second-largest distinct number.
For every number:
1. If it is greater than highest, move highest into secondHighest.
2. Update highest.
3. Otherwise, if it is smaller than highest but greater than secondHighest, update secondHighest.
B. Plain Java
int[] arr = {10, 20, 5, 40, 30};

int highest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
int secondHighest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

for (int num : arr) {

    if (num > highest) {

        secondHighest = highest;
        highest = num;

    } else if (num > secondHighest && num < highest) {

        secondHighest = num;
    }
}

if (secondHighest == Integer.MIN_VALUE) {
    System.out.println("Second highest doesn't exist");
} else {
    System.out.println(secondHighest);
}


For production-quality handling, use a separate boolean or OptionalInt to distinguish a missing second value from an actual Integer.MIN_VALUE.
C. Dry run
Current value	Highest	Second highest
10	10	-∞
20	20	10
5	20	10
40	40	20
30	40	30

D. Java 8
int[] arr = {10, 20, 5, 40, 30};

OptionalInt result = Arrays.stream(arr)
        .distinct()
        .boxed()
        .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
        .skip(1)
        .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)
        .findFirst();

System.out.println(result.orElse(-1));


Here -1 is just a demonstration fallback; use isPresent() or orElseThrow() if -1 could itself be a valid answer.
E. Complexity
Solution	Time	Extra space
Single loop	O(n)	O(1)
Java 8 sorting	O(n log n)	O(n)

F. Choice of solution
Use the single-loop solution.

It is faster, uses constant extra space and demonstrates good problem-solving skills.

Important follow-up: What happens if the array is {10, 10, 10}?

There is no second-highest distinct number.

















